e following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$"] [17592186247221 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-6)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$"] [17592186247222 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${t}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}}_{{t}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{t}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{t}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [17592186247223 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$"] [17592186247225 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} 