}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186272106 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."] [17592186272107 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."] [17592186272108 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3(\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{1}=-5.\n$$"] [17592186272109 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{10}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"] [17592186272239 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{6} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$ is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is invertible”; $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ only."] [17592186272349 "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has $8$ pivot columns” only."] [17592186317166 "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}+{p}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}+{p}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}+{p}_{4}\\cdot {q}_{4}+{p}_{5}\\cdot {q}_{5} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-10-4-4-9-10 \\\\  & =-37\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317169 "Starting from the equation $u\\cdot v=12,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =12 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}\\cdot 2{x}^{-2}+4\\cdot (-8x)+12\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 2 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48+12 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ Since we need $x<6$, our final answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186317226 "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317387 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k+9 \\\\ 2k+18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k+9 &=0 \\\\ 2k+18 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-9.$"] [17592186317441 "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186317454 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$"] [17592186318978 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}^{′} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, only the $1$st column is a pivot column, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st column from the original matrix is ${g}_{1}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is ${{g}_{1}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"] [17592186318982 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186319342 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2k-8 \\\\ k+4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k-8 &=0 \\\\ k+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-4.$"] [17592186323706 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add }(-w)\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326761 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=6-16+10=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=-8+10=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=15-12-1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."] [17592186326826 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4})-{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=5$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"]}
